<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.9.3: Vertex Form Given a Vertex and Point</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02482</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.9.3: Vertex Form Given a Vertex and Point</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2ba85e56-02e9-452a-87ce-2a15c32f7fce</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
<div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b62c9150250bf52d6cc37569213ab516e0090dca">Desmos guide PDF</link> for tips on solving problems with the Desmos graphing calculator.</para>
</div>

<para>For questions 1 - 2, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at (5, 8) and also goes through the point \((-3, 6)\).</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise id="_58610f1c-5083-4788-b112-383c4e6b3229" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)
        \(6=a(-3-5)^2+8\)
        \(6=a(-3-5)^2+8\)
        \(6=a(-8)^2+8\)
        \(6=a(-8)^2+8\)
        \(-2=64a\)
        \(-2=64a\)
        \(a=\frac{-2}{64}=\frac{-1}{32}\)
        \(a=\frac{-2}{64}=\frac{-1}{32}\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{-1}{32}\).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#2-->
<!--Q#2-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Select the<emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(y=\frac{-1}{32}(x-5)^2+8\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<para>For questions 3 and 4, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((-4, 3)\) and also goes through the point \((2, 5)\).</para>
<!--Interaction End -->
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_3fb8876e-eb1f-4f46-99ef-2345f1eefa1b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)
        \(5=a(2-(-4))^2+3\)
        \(5=a(2-(-4))^2+3\)
        \(5=a(6)^2+3\)
        \(5=a(6)^2+3\)
        \(2=36a\)
        \(2=36a\)
        \(a=\frac{2}{36}=\frac{1}{18}\)
        \(a=\frac{2}{36}=\frac{1}{18}\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{1}{18}\).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#4-->
<!--Q#4-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Select the<emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(y=\frac{1}{18}(x+4)^2+3\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>For questions 5 - 6, write an equation in vertex form of the quadratic function that has a vertex at \((6, -2)\) and also goes through the point \((3, 1)\).</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="_672d6cf9-11c0-41b0-8896-377ea42f89d5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Find the value of \(a\) for vertex form.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>\(y=a(x-h)^2+k\)
        \(1=a(3-6)^2+(-2)\)
        \(1=a(3-6)^2+(-2)\)
        \(1=a(-3)^2-2\)
        \(1=a(-3)^2-2\)
        \(3=9a\)
        \(3=9a\)
        \(a=\frac{3}{9}=\frac{1}{3}\)
        \(a=\frac{3}{9}=\frac{1}{3}\)</para><para>Nice try! The correct answer is \(\frac{1}{3}\).</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Q#6-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the equation in vertex form.</para>
    <para>Select the<emphasis>Solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para>\(y=\frac{1}{3}(x-6)^2-2\)</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example>
<!--Interaction End -->


